-- Q1: medium / L2 — GASB 96 subscription-based IT arrangement recognition
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Riverdale enters into a three-year subscription agreement on July 1, 2026, for cloud-based financial management software. The city pays $90,000 annually in advance with no purchase option. The discount rate is 5%. Under GASB 96, how should Riverdale record this arrangement at inception?', '["Recognize a $90,000 operating expense in the general fund", "Record only the first year''s payment as a prepaid asset", "Recognize a subscription asset and a corresponding subscription liability", "Capitalize the full $270,000 as an intangible asset with no liability"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 96, subscription-based information technology arrangements (SBITAs) require governments to recognize an intangible right-to-use subscription asset and a corresponding subscription liability at the present value of future payments. This treatment parallels the lease accounting model under GASB 87. Choice A is incorrect because GASB 96 does not permit expense recognition for arrangements exceeding 12 months; the standard mandates capitalization of the right-to-use asset.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Evaluate blending vs discrete presentation (GASB 14/61)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Elmwood has a legally separate Building Authority that was created solely to issue bonds for city facilities. The Authority''s governing board is appointed by the city council, and the city is the sole beneficiary of the Authority''s debt-financed construction. The finance director must determine how to present the Authority in the city''s annual financial report. Under GASB 14 as amended by GASB 61, what is the most appropriate presentation method?', '["Discrete presentation because the Authority is a legally separate entity", "Blending because the Authority exclusively benefits the primary government", "Exclusion because the Authority has its own separate governing board", "Fiduciary fund presentation because the Authority holds resources for the city"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 14 (as amended by GASB 61), a component unit should be blended when it provides services exclusively or almost exclusively to the primary government, even if legally separate. Because the Building Authority exists solely to issue bonds for city facilities and the city is the sole beneficiary, blending is appropriate. Choice A is incorrect because legal separateness alone does not dictate discrete presentation; the nature of the relationship and exclusivity of services determine the presentation method.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Definition of deferred inflows of resources
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Concepts Statement No. 4, what are deferred inflows of resources?', '["Acquisitions of net assets applicable to future reporting periods", "Revenues that have been earned but not yet collected", "Long-term liabilities due beyond one year", "Assets restricted by external creditors"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB Concepts Statement No. 4, deferred inflows of resources represent acquisitions of net assets by the government that are applicable to a future reporting period. They are reported as a separate section on the statement of net position or balance sheet. Choice B is incorrect because earned but uncollected revenues are reported as receivables, not deferred inflows; deferred inflows specifically relate to resources acquired before the period to which they apply.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Evaluate proportionate share pension liability (GASB 68)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Greenfield County participates in a state-administered cost-sharing pension plan. The county''s proportionate share of the collective net pension liability is $18.4 million. The county''s pension contributions for the year are $2.1 million, and the measurement date differs from the county''s fiscal year-end by three months. The county controller argues that reporting the full $18.4 million liability overstates the county''s obligation because the county can withdraw from the plan. Under GASB 68, what is the best assessment of the controller''s position?', '["The controller is correct because participation is voluntary and the liability should be limited to contributions due", "The controller is correct because the three-month measurement lag makes the liability unreliable", "The controller is partially correct and should reduce the liability by the county''s cumulative contributions", "The controller is incorrect because GASB 68 requires recognition of the proportionate share regardless of withdrawal provisions"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 68, paragraphs 48-49, employers participating in cost-sharing plans must recognize their proportionate share of the collective net pension liability. The ability to withdraw from the plan does not reduce the reported liability. GASB 68 explicitly rejected limiting the liability to contributions due because the obligation reflects the employer''s share of benefits already earned by employees. Choice A is incorrect because voluntary participation does not override the proportionate share allocation required by the standard.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: easy / L2 — Apply major fund criteria (10% test)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Millbrook has a special revenue fund with total assets of $6 million. The town''s total governmental fund assets are $80 million. Under GASB 34, is this fund required to be reported as a major fund based on the asset criterion alone?', '["Yes, because all special revenue funds are automatically major funds", "No, because the fund''s assets are less than 10% of total governmental fund assets", "Yes, because any fund exceeding $5 million must be reported separately", "No, because only the general fund is required to be a major fund"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 76, a governmental fund is reported as a major fund if its total assets, liabilities, revenues, or expenditures are at least 10% of the corresponding total for all funds of the same category and at least 5% of the aggregate for all governmental and enterprise funds. With $6 million of $80 million (7.5%), the fund fails the 10% threshold. Choice D is incorrect because while the general fund is always major, other funds can qualify as major if they meet both percentage criteria.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Interfund elimination within same activity type
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Hartfield reports a $1.5 million interfund receivable in its general fund from a capital projects fund for a temporary advance. The city is preparing its government-wide financial statements. Under GASB 34, how should this interfund balance be reported in the government-wide statement of net position?', '["Eliminate the balance because both funds are within governmental activities", "Report the full $1.5 million as an internal balance between activities", "Reclassify the advance as a long-term receivable in the general fund", "Report the balance only if the advance will be repaid within one year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 58, interfund receivables and payables between funds of the same activity type must be eliminated in the government-wide financial statements. Because both the general fund and the capital projects fund are governmental funds, the $1.5 million interfund balance is eliminated during the conversion to government-wide statements. Choice B is incorrect because internal balances are used only for amounts between governmental activities and business-type activities, not between funds within the same activity column.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — GASB 87 lease liability calculation
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'On January 1, 2026, the Town of Briarwood enters a seven-year lease for office equipment with annual payments of $42,000 due at the end of each year. The town''s incremental borrowing rate is 4%. The present value of an ordinary annuity of $1 at 4% for seven years is 6.0021. Under GASB 87, what amount should Briarwood report as the initial lease liability?', '["$294,000", "$42,000", "$210,000", "$252,088"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 87, paragraph 18, a lessee recognizes a lease liability at the present value of future lease payments, discounted at the rate the lessor charges or, if not known, the lessee''s incremental borrowing rate. The calculation is $42,000 multiplied by 6.0021 which equals $252,088. Choice A ($294,000) is incorrect because it represents the total undiscounted payments over seven years; GASB 87 requires discounting future payments to present value rather than summing nominal amounts.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Special assessment bond accounting
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Oakville levies a $2 million special assessment against property owners to finance sidewalk improvements. The city issues $2 million in bonds backed by the assessment, and the city is obligated in some manner for the debt. Under GASB standards, how should Oakville account for the bond proceeds and construction activity?', '["Record both in the general fund as a routine capital expenditure", "Record both in an agency fund since the assessments belong to property owners", "Record the bond proceeds and construction in a capital projects fund and the debt in the government-wide statements", "Record the bonds as a proprietary fund liability and construction as an enterprise fund expenditure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB Codification Section S40, when a government is obligated in some manner for special assessment debt, the transactions are reported in governmental funds. Bond proceeds and related construction expenditures are recorded in a capital projects fund, while the long-term debt appears in the government-wide statements because governmental funds do not report long-term liabilities. Choice B is incorrect because agency (now custodial) fund treatment applies only when the government is not obligated in any manner for the debt.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Definition of MD&A
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, what is management''s discussion and analysis (MD&A)?', '["An optional letter from the government''s chief executive to taxpayers", "Required supplementary information that introduces the basic financial statements", "A component of the notes to the financial statements", "An independent auditor''s analysis of government financial performance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, paragraphs 8-11, management''s discussion and analysis is required supplementary information (RSI) that precedes the basic financial statements. MD&A provides an objective and easily readable overview of the government''s financial activities. Choice A is incorrect because MD&A is required, not optional, and it follows a specific content framework prescribed by GASB 34 rather than being a discretionary communication from the executive.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Proprietary fund measurement focus
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'What measurement focus do proprietary funds use under GASB standards?', '["Economic resources measurement focus", "Current financial resources measurement focus", "Cash basis measurement focus", "Modified cash measurement focus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 92, proprietary funds use the economic resources measurement focus and the accrual basis of accounting, the same approach used by commercial enterprises. This means all assets and liabilities, both current and long-term, are reported. Choice B is incorrect because the current financial resources measurement focus applies to governmental funds, which report only current assets and current liabilities rather than the full set of economic resources.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — GASB 77 tax abatement disclosure
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Woodhaven granted a 10-year property tax abatement to Pinecrest Industries valued at $350,000 per year to encourage development of a manufacturing facility. The city is preparing its financial statements for fiscal year 2026. Under GASB 77, what is the required treatment?', '["Record a $350,000 expenditure in the general fund for the foregone revenue", "Recognize a $3.5 million contingent liability for the total abatement commitment", "Disclose the tax abatement program, including the gross dollar amount of taxes abated during the period", "Report the abatement as a reduction to the tax levy with no additional disclosure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 77, paragraphs 4-7, governments must disclose information about tax abatement agreements, including a brief description of the program, the gross dollar amount of taxes abated during the reporting period, and any commitments made as part of the agreement. GASB 77 requires note disclosure, not recognition as an expenditure or liability. Choice A is incorrect because the standard addresses disclosure requirements only; the foregone revenue is never collected, so there is no expenditure to record.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Analyze GASB 87 effect on net position
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The County of Mapleton implements GASB 87 for the first time in fiscal year 2026 and recognizes $14 million in right-of-use lease assets and $15.2 million in lease liabilities for operating leases previously reported only in the notes. What is the most likely effect on the county''s government-wide statement of net position?', '["Total net position increases because new assets are recognized", "Total net position decreases because lease liabilities exceed the corresponding lease assets", "Total net position is unchanged because assets and liabilities offset exactly", "Total net position increases because previously off-balance-sheet items are now capitalized"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 87, paragraphs 16-18, lease assets are measured at the present value of payments plus any prepayments, while lease liabilities equal the present value of future payments. When liabilities exceed assets (as occurs when initial direct costs and prepayments are minimal), net position decreases upon implementation. The $1.2 million excess of liabilities over assets reduces net position. Choice C is incorrect because assets and liabilities only offset exactly when there are prepayments equal to the difference, which is not the typical case.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Evaluate fund balance classification hierarchy (GASB 54)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Grandview receives a $4.2 million state grant with the legislative mandate that funds be used exclusively for emergency preparedness. The city council passes a resolution further restricting $1.5 million of the grant for a new alert system. The city''s finance director classifies the entire $4.2 million as committed fund balance because the city council''s action is the highest level of decision-making authority. Under GASB 54, what is the correct evaluation of the finance director''s classification?', '["The classification is incorrect; the entire $4.2 million should be restricted because the constraint is imposed by the state", "The classification is correct because the city council''s resolution supersedes the state mandate", "The classification is partially correct; $1.5 million should be committed and $2.7 million restricted", "The classification is incorrect; $4.2 million should be assigned because grants are not legally binding constraints"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 54, paragraphs 6-8, fund balance classifications follow a hierarchy where external constraints (from creditors, grantors, or laws of other governments) result in restricted classification, regardless of any additional internal actions. Because the state legislature imposed the mandate, the entire $4.2 million meets the restricted definition. The city council''s resolution does not elevate the classification. Choice C is incorrect because the council''s action cannot reclassify externally restricted resources to a lower classification level.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Evaluate component unit reporting method (GASB 14/61)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Lakeside County has a legally separate public library system whose five-member board is appointed by the county commission. The library receives 40% of its funding from county appropriations and 60% from an independent endowment and state grants. The library sets its own budget, hires its own staff, and can issue debt without county approval. The county auditor must determine how to present the library. Under GASB 14 as amended by GASB 61, what is the most appropriate treatment?', '["Exclude the library because it is financially independent from the county", "Blend the library because the county appoints a voting majority of its board", "Report the library as a discretely presented component unit because the county appoints the board majority and can impose its will", "Report the library in a fiduciary fund because the county holds funds on the library''s behalf"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 14 (as amended by GASB 61), paragraphs 21-22, a legally separate entity is a component unit if the primary government appoints a voting majority of the board and can impose its will or the entity is fiscally dependent. The county''s appointment power over the library board establishes the component unit relationship. Discrete presentation is appropriate because the library serves the public, not exclusively the primary government. Choice B is incorrect because blending requires the component unit to serve the primary government exclusively, not merely having an appointed board.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Interfund services provided and used
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Crestwood''s internal service fund provides fleet maintenance to the police department at a billed rate of $180,000 per quarter. During fiscal year 2026, the police department''s general fund paid $720,000 to the internal service fund for these services. Under GASB 34, how should the $720,000 be reported in the general fund?', '["As a transfer out to the internal service fund", "As a reduction to fund balance with no expenditure recognition", "As a capital outlay expenditure for fleet assets", "As an expenditure for fleet maintenance services"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 112, interfund services provided and used—where one fund provides goods or services to another for a price approximating external exchange value—are reported as expenditures in the purchasing fund and revenues in the selling fund. The police department''s general fund recognizes the $720,000 as an expenditure. Choice A is incorrect because transfers represent non-exchange flows between funds, whereas services provided and used are quasi-external transactions recorded as revenues and expenditures.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Required supplementary information
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB standards, which of the following is classified as required supplementary information?', '["The statement of net position", "Notes to the financial statements", "Budgetary comparison schedules for the general fund", "The combining statements for nonmajor funds"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 130, budgetary comparison schedules for the general fund and each major special revenue fund with a legally adopted annual budget are required supplementary information (RSI). RSI accompanies but is not part of the basic financial statements. Choice A is incorrect because the statement of net position is a basic financial statement, not supplementary information; RSI supplements the basic statements with additional context such as budgetary comparisons and pension trend data.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Analyze capital asset reporting: depreciation vs modified approach
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Silverton purchases a $2.8 million road grader from its general fund and also maintains $45 million of highway infrastructure under the modified approach. The finance director asks how these two capital asset categories will be treated differently on the government-wide statement of net position. What is the most likely difference in reporting treatment?', '["The road grader is depreciated over its useful life, while the infrastructure reported under the modified approach is not depreciated", "Both the road grader and the infrastructure are depreciated using the same method", "The road grader is expensed immediately, while the infrastructure is capitalized", "The infrastructure is reported at fair value, while the road grader is reported at historical cost"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, paragraphs 23-26, governments may elect the modified approach for eligible infrastructure assets, under which the assets are not depreciated if the government maintains them at or above a condition level it establishes. Other capital assets, such as the road grader, follow standard depreciation methods. Choice B is incorrect because the modified approach is an explicit alternative to depreciation for infrastructure assets that meet condition assessment requirements, creating a distinct reporting treatment.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — GASB 72 fair value hierarchy levels
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 72, what are the three levels of the fair value measurement hierarchy?', '["Historical cost, replacement cost, and net realizable value", "Entry price, exit price, and settlement price", "Market approach, cost approach, and income approach", "Quoted prices in active markets, observable inputs, and unobservable inputs"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 72, paragraphs 46-59, the fair value hierarchy consists of three levels: Level 1 (quoted prices in active markets for identical assets), Level 2 (observable inputs other than quoted prices), and Level 3 (unobservable inputs based on the government''s own assumptions). Choice C is incorrect because market, cost, and income approaches are valuation techniques used to measure fair value, not levels within the hierarchy; the hierarchy classifies the inputs to those techniques by their observability.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Budgetary comparison schedule presentation
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Ferndale adopted its general fund budget on a cash basis, but reports its financial statements on the modified accrual basis under GAAP. Actual revenues on the budgetary basis are $32 million, and accrual adjustments add $1.8 million for receivables expected within 60 days. Under GASB 34, how should the budgetary comparison schedule present this information?', '["Report only the GAAP-basis actual amount of $33.8 million", "Report the budgetary-basis actual of $32 million alongside the budget amounts, with a reconciliation to GAAP", "Adjust the budget amounts to the modified accrual basis for comparability", "Report the $1.8 million difference as a prior period adjustment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, paragraphs 130-131, the budgetary comparison schedule must present both the original and final budget amounts alongside actual results on the budgetary basis. When the budgetary basis differs from GAAP, a reconciliation to GAAP amounts must be provided either on the schedule or in the notes. Choice A is incorrect because presenting only GAAP-basis amounts would obscure the comparison with the legally adopted budget, defeating the purpose of budgetary accountability.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Government-mandated nonexchange definition (GASB 33)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Statement No. 33, what is a government-mandated nonexchange transaction?', '["A transaction where a higher-level government requires a lower-level government to perform an activity and provides funding", "A transaction where a government voluntarily provides resources to another entity", "A tax imposed directly on citizens by the receiving government", "A fee charged for services rendered by a government agency"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 33, paragraph 7, government-mandated nonexchange transactions occur when a higher-level government provides resources to a lower-level government and requires it to use those resources for a specific purpose. Federal grants for Medicaid or education are common examples. Choice C is incorrect because taxes imposed directly on citizens by the receiving government are classified as either derived tax revenues or imposed nonexchange revenues, not government-mandated transactions.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Analyze pension deferred outflows/inflows effect on net position
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Maplewood Township reports $3.6 million in deferred outflows of resources and $2.1 million in deferred inflows of resources related to its defined benefit pension plan under GASB 68. The actuary reports that the difference between projected and actual investment earnings was $1.4 million unfavorable. What is the most likely effect of these pension-related deferred amounts on the government-wide financial statements?', '["Net position increases by $1.4 million because unfavorable investment returns are deferred indefinitely", "Pension expense is reduced because deferred inflows exceed deferred outflows", "Net position is $1.5 million higher than it would be without the deferrals because deferred outflows exceed deferred inflows", "The effect is limited to the notes to the financial statements with no balance sheet impact"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 68, paragraphs 33-37, deferred outflows increase net position while deferred inflows decrease it. With $3.6 million in deferred outflows and $2.1 million in deferred inflows, the net effect increases net position by $1.5 million relative to immediate recognition. These deferrals smooth pension expense over future periods. Choice B is incorrect because the deferred outflows ($3.6 million) exceed deferred inflows ($2.1 million), resulting in a net positive effect on net position rather than a reduction in pension expense.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Analyze GASB 49 pollution remediation liability
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Brookhaven discovers contaminated soil at a former industrial site it acquired for a park. Environmental consultants estimate remediation costs between $2.5 million and $4.8 million, with $3.2 million being the most likely amount. The city has not yet begun cleanup. Under GASB 49, what is the most likely effect on the government-wide financial statements when an obligating event has occurred?', '["No liability is recognized until cleanup begins and costs are incurred", "A contingent liability of $4.8 million is disclosed in the notes only", "A liability equal to the midpoint of the range ($3.65 million) is recognized", "A liability of $3.2 million is recognized because it is the expected amount within the range"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 49, paragraphs 15-18, when an obligating event such as imminent endangerment occurs, a government must recognize the expected amount of the pollution remediation liability. When a specific amount within the range is identified as the expected cost, that amount is used rather than the minimum or midpoint. The $3.2 million most-likely estimate represents the expected amount. Choice C is incorrect because the midpoint method applies only when no single amount within the range is better than any other estimate.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — GASB 54 committed fund balance modification authority
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'On November 15, 2026, the Ridgeway Township board of trustees passes a resolution committing $800,000 of general fund balance for future technology upgrades. The board''s policy designates the board of trustees as the highest level of decision-making authority. On December 1, the township manager reallocates $200,000 of the committed amount to office renovations. Under GASB 54, what is the committed fund balance at December 31, 2026?', '["$600,000 because the township manager''s reallocation reduces the committed amount", "$800,000 because only the board of trustees can modify committed fund balance", "$0 because committed fund balance cannot be established for future projects", "$200,000 because only the reallocated portion remains committed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 54, paragraph 10, committed fund balance can be established and modified only by the government''s highest level of decision-making authority, which here is the board of trustees. A township manager lacks the authority to modify committed fund balance. Therefore, the original $800,000 commitment remains intact at year-end. Choice A is incorrect because the township manager''s reallocation is invalid under GASB 54; the same formal action by the same body that committed the resources must also be used to modify the commitment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L3 — Analyze derivative hedging classification (GASB 53)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Metro Transit Authority enters into an interest rate swap on $25 million of its variable-rate revenue bonds. The swap converts the variable rate to a fixed 3.8%. At fiscal year-end, the swap has a negative fair value of $1.1 million. The Authority determines the swap qualifies as an effective hedging derivative under GASB 53. How does the hedging classification affect the reporting of the $1.1 million negative fair value?', '["The $1.1 million is reported as a deferred outflow of resources rather than an investment loss", "The $1.1 million is recognized immediately as investment revenue in the statement of activities", "The $1.1 million is excluded from the financial statements because the hedge is effective", "The $1.1 million is reported as a reduction to the carrying amount of the bonds"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 53, paragraphs 20-24, when a derivative instrument qualifies as an effective hedge, changes in fair value are reported as deferred outflows or deferred inflows of resources rather than as investment gains or losses. The $1.1 million negative fair value produces a deferred outflow because the government would owe that amount upon termination. Choice B is incorrect because immediate recognition in the statement of activities applies only to investment derivatives that do not qualify as effective hedges; the hedging designation specifically defers the fair value change.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Analyze special revenue fund revenue composition (GASB 54)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Ridgemont reports a special revenue fund for recreation programs funded by a dedicated 1% local sales tax. During fiscal year 2026, the sales tax generates $4.2 million, but the city also transfers $3.5 million from the general fund to cover program expansion costs. What is the most likely effect on the fund''s classification under GASB 54?', '["The fund retains its special revenue classification because it was originally established with a restricted revenue source", "The fund must be reclassified as a capital projects fund due to the expansion spending", "The fund''s classification may be questioned because a substantial portion of revenue is now from unrestricted general fund transfers rather than restricted or committed sources", "The fund must be reclassified as a permanent fund because the sales tax is ongoing"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 54, paragraph 30, special revenue funds should report resources restricted or committed for purposes other than capital projects or debt service, and the restricted or committed revenues should constitute a substantial portion of the fund''s inflows. When general fund transfers ($3.5 million) approach the restricted revenue ($4.2 million), the fund''s classification becomes questionable. Choice A is incorrect because GASB 54 requires ongoing evaluation of the revenue composition, not merely the original establishment purpose.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Pension trust fund purpose
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB standards, what is the purpose of a pension trust fund?', '["To account for general government employee salary expenditures", "To account for resources held in trust for the members and beneficiaries of a pension plan", "To account for debt service payments on pension obligation bonds", "To record the employer''s annual pension contribution expense"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 70, pension trust funds are fiduciary funds used to report resources held in trust for pension plan members and beneficiaries. These funds account for plan assets, contributions received, investment income, and benefit payments. Choice A is incorrect because employee salary expenditures are reported in the governmental fund that pays the salaries, not in the pension trust fund, which holds and manages the accumulated plan assets.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Evaluate internal service fund premium overcharging
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Westbrook established an internal service fund for workers'' compensation self-insurance five years ago. The fund charges premiums to operating departments based on claims history. The fund reports assets of $8.3 million, liabilities of $6.7 million (including incurred-but-not-reported claims), and accumulated surplus of $1.6 million from consistently charging premiums above cost. The city auditor questions whether the premium charges are reasonable. Under GASB standards, what is the best evaluation?', '["The practice is acceptable because internal service funds are designed to accumulate reserves for future claims", "The surplus should be returned immediately to the general fund as excess revenue", "The fund should be reclassified as an enterprise fund due to the accumulated surplus", "The practice is problematic because charges significantly exceeding costs distort expenditures in the purchasing funds and overstate the internal service fund''s net position"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 60, and GASB Codification Section Si05, internal service fund charges should approximate the cost of providing the service. Persistently excessive charges inflate expenditures in the departments paying premiums and create artificial surpluses in the internal service fund. During government-wide consolidation, these overcharges must be eliminated. Choice A is incorrect because while reasonable reserves are appropriate, systematic overcharging distorts both fund-level and government-wide reporting under GASB guidance.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Analyze capital projects fund surplus disposition
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Ashland completes a $12 million library construction project financed entirely by general obligation bonds reported in a capital projects fund. The project comes in $340,000 under budget, and the bond indenture requires that unused proceeds be used for debt retirement. The finance director proposes transferring the $340,000 to the general fund. What is the most likely effect of the bond indenture restriction?', '["The $340,000 must be transferred to the debt service fund because the bond indenture restricts the use of unused proceeds", "The $340,000 can be transferred to the general fund because the project is complete", "The $340,000 remains in the capital projects fund as restricted fund balance indefinitely", "The $340,000 is returned to the bondholders as an early principal payment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB standards and general bond covenants, when a bond indenture specifies that unused proceeds must be applied to debt retirement, the restriction governs the disposition of surplus funds. The $340,000 must be transferred to the debt service fund to comply with the indenture. Choice B is incorrect because the finance director cannot override the bond indenture''s legal restriction; external constraints from creditors take precedence over management''s preferred disposition of remaining funds.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Analyze major component unit condensed financial information
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The County of Sterling has three discretely presented component units with combined total assets of $85 million. One component unit has total assets of $62 million while the other two each have less than $12 million. The county is preparing the notes to the government-wide financial statements. What is the most likely implication for the note disclosures under GASB 34?', '["All three component units receive identical note disclosure treatment regardless of size", "The two smaller component units can be excluded from the notes entirely", "The larger component unit may require individual condensed financial information in the notes because it represents a significant portion of the aggregate", "The county must combine all three component units into a single disclosure line"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 126, governments should present condensed financial information for each major component unit in the notes. A component unit representing 73% of the aggregate component unit total assets is significant enough to warrant individual disclosure. This parallels the major fund concept for governmental and enterprise funds. Choice A is incorrect because GASB 34 permits aggregation of nonmajor component units in a single column while requiring major component units to be presented individually with condensed financial data.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — GASB 87 short-term lease exception
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 87, what is the maximum possible term for a lease to qualify as a short-term lease?', '["6 months", "24 months", "36 months", "12 months"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 87, paragraph 16, a short-term lease has a maximum possible term of 12 months or less, including any options to extend. Short-term leases are exempt from the capitalization requirements, and payments are recognized as expenses or expenditures in the period incurred. Choice B is incorrect because the 24-month threshold has no basis in GASB 87; the standard specifically establishes 12 months as the cutoff, beyond which the lessee must recognize a lease liability and a right-to-use asset.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
